  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-LKA102</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">National strategy formulated to respond to technological advancements and other productive market demands  for population at large</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&quot;Significant skills mismatches between education outputs and employer needs, the lack of formal recognition for migrant workers&apos; skills, and inadequate systems to respond to technological change and the growing care economy are among the  key challenges in the labor market in Sri Lanka. The most vulnerable groups include unemployed youth seeking relevant skills, returning migrant workers requiring certification, women facing employment barriers, and micro, small, and medium-sized enterprises needing workforce development. 

The ILO&apos;s strategy to respond to these challenges is implemented through a tripartite approach involving government agencies such as the Ministry of Labour, the Tertiary and Vocational Education Commission (TVEC), and the National Apprenticeship and Industrial Training Authority (NAITA), which lead policy development and execution. Employers and industry associations help align training with labor market demands, while trade unions protect workers&#x2019; rights.  Additionally, collaboration with ministries responsible for women and child affairs and civil society organizations aims to formalize care work, extend social protections for domestic and care workers, and address gender gaps in decent work opportunities.&quot;</narrative>
  </description>
